= Register-transfer level — Simple =

<onlyinclude>'''Register-transfer level''' ('''RTL''') is a way of describing how a digital circuit stores information and changes it. A register remembers bits; logic calculates what should happen next; a clock can determine when the new value is stored. RTL describes the circuit's intended behavior without specifying every transistor or wire.</onlyinclude>

== Example: a counter ==
Imagine a four-bit counter with a clock and an enable signal. On an enabled clock edge it counts up by one; when it reaches 15, the next increment wraps to 0. With enable off, the register keeps its value. The wraparound comes from the four-bit width, not from a universal property of counters.

== What RTL is not ==
RTL is not the same as a hardware description language. A language such as SystemVerilog can express RTL, simulation-only tests, and other models. Nor does an RTL description prove that a fabricated circuit will meet speed, power, or reliability requirements.

== From description to hardware ==
Engineers can simulate the design, compare it with requirements, synthesize a gate-level implementation, and check timing. Different stages can expose different defects.

